::STAT_U01_PT_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U01_PT_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A survey asks students to report their favorite school lunch. What type of data is collected?
{
~A. Numerical#No numerical measurement or count is recorded for each student.
~B. Bivariate numerical#Only one variable is recorded, and it is categorical.
~C. Relative frequency#Relative frequency is a summary proportion, not the original data type.
=D. Categorical#Favorite lunch is a category label, not a measured amount.
}

::STAT_U01_PT_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U01_PT_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
A data set records hours studied and quiz score for each student. Which display is most appropriate for exploring the relationship?
{
=A. Scatterplot#A scatterplot shows the relationship between two numerical variables.
~B. Pie chart#A pie chart shows parts of a whole for one categorical variable.
~C. Frequency table for one category#A one-way frequency table would not show the relationship between two numerical variables.
~D. Box plot of one variable only#A single box plot would summarize one numerical variable, not the relationship.
}

::STAT_U01_PT_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U01_PT_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A histogram interval from 20 to 29 minutes has a bar height of 7. What does the height represent?
{
~A. The average time is 7 minutes#The bar height is a frequency, not the mean.
=B. Seven values fall in the 20 to 29 minute interval#Histogram bar height shows the frequency for that interval.
~C. The interval is 7 minutes wide#The interval width comes from the endpoints, not the bar height.
~D. Seven categories were surveyed#A histogram shows numerical intervals, not category labels.
}

::STAT_U01_PT_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U01_PT_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A relative frequency table shows that 35% of Grade 9 students ride the bus. In a segmented bar graph, how much of the Grade 9 bar should the bus segment occupy?
{
~A. 65%#65% is the complement, not the listed bus relative frequency.
~B. 3.5%#This moves the decimal incorrectly.
~C. 100%#The whole bar is 100%, but bus is only one segment.
=D. 35%#Segment length should match the relative frequency for that category.
}

::STAT_U01_PT_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U01_PT_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which display is usually appropriate for one numerical variable such as daily step counts?
{
=A. Dot plot#A dot plot can show the distribution of one numerical variable.
~B. Two-way table#A two-way table organizes two categorical variables.
~C. Pie chart#A pie chart shows categorical parts of a whole.
~D. Segmented bar graph#A segmented bar graph compares categorical proportions across groups.
}

::STAT_U01_PT_Q06::
Question ID: STAT_U01_PT_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
A bar chart shows favorite study method. The tallest bar is online videos. What conclusion is supported?
{
~A. Online videos caused students to earn higher grades#The display shows preference counts, not cause and effect.
~B. Every student chose online videos#Other bars may still have responses.
=C. Online videos had the greatest number of responses among the listed methods#The tallest category bar represents the greatest frequency.
~D. Online videos is a numerical variable#Favorite study method is categorical.
}

::STAT_U01_PT_Q07::
Question ID: STAT_U01_PT_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
Why are relative frequencies useful when comparing groups with different totals?
{
~A. They remove the need for category labels#Category labels are still needed to interpret the display.
=B. They compare proportions fairly across groups#Relative frequencies account for different group sizes.
~C. They prove that one category caused another#Relative frequencies describe data; they do not prove causation.
~D. They make every raw count equal#Relative frequencies do not change the original counts.
}

::STAT_U01_PT_Q08::
Question ID: STAT_U01_PT_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.1
Question:
Which data situation is bivariate categorical?
{
~A. Heights of students in inches#This is one numerical variable.
=B. Grade level and preferred lunch option#Both variables are categorical.
~C. Study time and test score#These are two numerical variables.
~D. Number of pets owned by each student#This is one numerical variable.
}

::STAT_U01_PT_Q09::
Question ID: STAT_U01_PT_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.STQ.2
Question:
Before interpreting a graph, which components should be checked first?
{
~A. Only the color of the bars#Color alone does not define the meaning of the data.
~B. Only the largest value#The largest value needs labels, units, and context to be meaningful.
=C. Title, labels, units, scale, and context#These components explain what the display and quantities mean.
~D. Only whether the graph looks neat#Visual neatness does not guarantee correct interpretation.
}

::STAT_U01_PT_Q10::
Question ID: STAT_U01_PT_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.DVD.3
Question:
A segmented bar graph shows Grade 10 has a larger car segment than Grade 9. What is a supported interpretation?
{
=A. Grade 10 has a greater proportion of students choosing car#A larger segment means a larger relative frequency for that category.
~B. Grade 10 must have more total students#Segmented percent bars compare proportions, not necessarily total counts.
~C. Car choice caused students to be in Grade 10#The graph does not show cause and effect.
~D. No Grade 9 students chose car#A smaller segment may still represent some Grade 9 students.
}
